WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.559
When you're building agentic systems, one of the earliest design decisions you hit

2
00:00:04.559 --> 00:00:10.239
is where state actually lives, inside the active conversation or in external systems.

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.319
For this video, we'll look at when each approach is correct,

4
00:00:14.319 --> 00:00:19.840
how to design stores for agentic systems, and how hybrid approaches combine both tiers.

5
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:20.719
Let's get into it.

6
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:25.040
Every fact an agent uses has to live somewhere,

7
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.000
and where it lives changes what the agent can do with it.

8
00:00:28.479 --> 00:00:32.959
In-context state is everything the agent knows from the current conversation.

9
00:00:32.959 --> 00:00:35.599
Messages, tool results, retrieved documents,

10
00:00:35.599 --> 00:00:40.159
all held directly in the context window the model is reading.

11
00:00:40.159 --> 00:00:42.400
It's instant, and it's fragile.

12
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.840
External memory is state stored outside the model.

13
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.560
In key-value databases, relational tables, or vector indexes.

14
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.000
It persists across sessions and survives truncation.

15
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.880
But every read and write costs a tool call.

16
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.639
Semantic retrieval is how you query the vector side of that.

17
00:01:00.639 --> 00:01:06.559
By meaning, rather than exact key, using embeddings to return the most relevant stored chunks.

18
00:01:06.559 --> 00:01:11.760
Speed, durability, and query model are the axes you'll be trading off all lesson.

19
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.879
In-context state is the simplest form of agent memory.

20
00:01:16.879 --> 00:01:19.599
It's everything the model reads during inference.

21
00:01:19.599 --> 00:01:24.639
The conversation history, tool results, injected documents, and the system prompt.

22
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.160
The key advantage is that there's no retrieval step.

23
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.879
The model reads in-context state as part of normal inference with zero additional latency.

24
00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.959
But there's a hard limit.

25
00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:39.760
Everything has to fit in the context window.

26
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.360
And that limit is fixed.

27
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.239
When the window fills up, the system has to truncate.

28
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.800
Older content gets dropped.

29
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.959
And when the session ends, in-context state is gone entirely.

30
00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:53.120
Unless something wrote it to an external store.

31
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.680
Fast and convenient, but not durable at all for anything that needs to survive an interruption.

32
00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:06.080
External memory is state stored in systems the agent can read and write via tool calls.

33
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.080
Key value stores, relational databases, and vector indexes all fall into this category.

34
00:02:12.639 --> 00:02:14.800
The big advantage is persistence.

35
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.119
External memory survives context truncation and process restarts.

36
00:02:19.119 --> 00:02:23.600
A session that ends or crashes doesn't lose the state that was written out.

37
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.720
External stores are also queryable.

38
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.960
A key value store lets you look up state by identifier.

39
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.639
And a vector index lets you retrieve by semantic similarity.

40
00:02:34.639 --> 00:02:36.399
The cost is latency.

41
00:02:36.399 --> 00:02:39.919
Every read and write requires a tool call, which adds time.

42
00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:42.479
In-context reads are free.

43
00:02:42.479 --> 00:02:43.759
External reads aren't.

44
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.720
That trade-off is the central tension in state design for agentic systems.

45
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.440
And the right balance depends on what the state needs to do.

46
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:56.559
With both forms defined, the comparison comes down to speed,

47
00:02:56.559 --> 00:02:58.880
durability, and how you query the state.

48
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.919
In-context state is instant.

49
00:03:01.919 --> 00:03:04.800
The model reads it as part of normal inference.

50
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.880
No tool call, no latency.

51
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.520
But it's bounded by the context window.

52
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.720
When the window fills up, content gets truncated.

53
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.119
When the session ends, the state is gone.

54
00:03:15.679 --> 00:03:18.080
External memory is the opposite trade-off.

55
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.639
Every read and write costs a tool call, which adds latency.

56
00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:27.279
But the state persists across sessions, survives truncation,

57
00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:30.639
and can be queried by key or by semantic similarity.

58
00:03:31.279 --> 00:03:33.360
The two aren't interchangeable.

59
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.160
In-context is right for transient, hot state that only matters this turn.

60
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.919
External is right for durable state that needs to outlive the current context.

61
00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:47.839
With that framing in hand, let's look at the kinds of external stores

62
00:03:47.839 --> 00:03:49.199
actually used in production.

63
00:03:50.479 --> 00:03:54.720
The three main types of external stores each suit different access patterns.

64
00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:57.839
Key value stores are the simplest.

65
00:03:57.839 --> 00:04:01.600
They let you look up state by an exact identifier instantly.

66
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.360
User profiles, task status, and configuration all fit this pattern well.

67
00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.160
Relational databases support structured queries, joins, and filtering.

68
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.440
They're the right choice for audit logs, records,

69
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.119
and anything that needs complex queries across multiple fields.

70
00:04:19.679 --> 00:04:23.119
Vector indexes support semantic similarity search.

71
00:04:23.119 --> 00:04:29.440
They're used when the agent needs to retrieve information by meaning rather than by exact key,

72
00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.959
like finding the most relevant prior conversation or the closest document to a current query.

73
00:04:34.959 --> 00:04:38.720
Choosing the right store type is about matching the store's query model

74
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.920
to how the agent actually needs to access the data.

75
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.799
Using a key value store when you need semantic retrieval is a design mismatch.

76
00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:53.119
The core decision question for where state lives is whether it needs to survive

77
00:04:53.119 --> 00:04:54.959
beyond the current context.

78
00:04:54.959 --> 00:05:00.000
Hot state is state that's actively changing this turn and referenced frequently.

79
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.079
It belongs in context where the model can read it with zero latency.

80
00:05:04.079 --> 00:05:08.000
Cold state is state that's rarely needed during active execution,

81
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.480
but has to persist across sessions.

82
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.559
It belongs in external storage.

83
00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:18.720
Reconstructable state is state that can be derived from source data or tool calls if it's lost.

84
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.959
There's often no reason to store it at all.

85
00:05:20.959 --> 00:05:23.839
The default should be in context for speed.

86
00:05:23.839 --> 00:05:28.720
Escalate to external storage only when there's a specific durability requirement

87
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.720
that in context storage can't meet.

88
00:05:31.279 --> 00:05:34.559
Over persisting state adds latency cost without adding value

89
00:05:34.559 --> 00:05:36.640
when the information could just be regenerated.

90
00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.920
Four questions help determine whether state needs to survive beyond the active context.

91
00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:47.920
First, if the session ended right now, is this state recoverable without external storage?

92
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.239
If no, it needs to be persisted.

93
00:05:50.799 --> 00:05:55.200
Second, does the agent need to retrieve this state by meaning or similarity

94
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.040
rather than by exact key?

95
00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.519
If yes, a vector store is the right choice.

96
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.720
Third, does another session or another user need to read this state later?

97
00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.480
Cross-session sharing requires external persistence by definition.

98
00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.000
Fourth, can this state be rebuilt cheaply from source data or tool calls?

99
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.440
If yes, it may not need explicit storage at all.

100
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.760
Working through these four questions at design time prevents both under persisting,

101
00:06:22.399 --> 00:06:26.959
important state, and over persisting state that adds latency cost without any real benefit.

102
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.279
Most production agents use both in context and external memory at the same time.

103
00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:37.839
The pattern is a hot tier and a cold tier.

104
00:06:37.839 --> 00:06:41.519
The hot tier keeps actively changing state in context.

105
00:06:41.519 --> 00:06:45.600
Things like the current steps inputs and outputs, the active plan,

106
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.079
and any tool results from this turn.

107
00:06:48.079 --> 00:06:52.320
The model reads all of this during inference with no extra latency.

108
00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.480
The cold tier persists completed results, configuration, and history to external stores.

109
00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.519
When a session resumes after an interruption,

110
00:07:01.519 --> 00:07:07.200
the agent loads only the minimal state it needs from the cold tier to pick up where it left off

111
00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:08.399
rather than loading everything.

112
00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.119
Hybrid design combines the speed advantage of in-context access

113
00:07:13.119 --> 00:07:17.920
with the durability advantage of external storage without paying the latency cost

114
00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:21.839
of externalizing state that doesn't need to leave the context window.

115
00:07:23.519 --> 00:07:25.279
State has a temperature.

116
00:07:25.279 --> 00:07:28.480
What the model needs every turn stays hot,

117
00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.600
right in the conversation where reading costs nothing.

118
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.519
What has to outlive a restart is in cold storage.

119
00:07:36.079 --> 00:07:39.200
It goes into a store you query on demand.

120
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.440
The design holds on a single question you ask of each piece of state.

121
00:07:44.079 --> 00:07:46.320
Does this need to survive an interruption?

122
00:07:47.119 --> 00:07:48.640
That wraps up this lesson.

123
00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:50.000
I'll see you in the next one.

